.SS " t\&.Union[\fBUndefined\fP, t\&.Any] jinja2\&.filters\&.do_attr ('Environment' environment, t\&.Any obj, str
 name)"

.PP
.nf
Get an attribute of an object\&. ``foo|attr('bar')`` works like
``foo\&.bar``, but returns undefined instead of falling back to ``foo['bar']``
if the attribute doesn't exist\&.

See :ref:`Notes on subscriptions <notes-on-subscriptions>` for more details\&.

.fi
.PP
 
.SS " 't\&.Iterator[t\&.List[\fBV\fP]]' jinja2\&.filters\&.do_batch ('t\&.Iterable[\fBV\fP]' value, int linecount, 't\&.Optional[\fBV\fP]'  fill_with = \fCNone\fP)"

.PP
.nf
A filter that batches items\&. It works pretty much like `slice`
just the other way round\&. It returns a list of lists with the
given number of items\&. If you provide a second parameter this
is used to fill up missing items\&. See this example:

\&.\&. sourcecode:: html+jinja

    <table>
    {%- for row in items|batch(3, '&nbsp;') %}
      <tr>
      {%- for column in row %}
        <td>{{ column }}</td>
      {%- endfor %}
      </tr>
    {%- endfor %}
    </table>

.fi
.PP
 
.SS " str jinja2\&.filters\&.do_capitalize (str s)"

.PP
.nf
Capitalize a value\&. The first character will be uppercase, all others
lowercase\&.

.fi
.PP
 
.SS " str jinja2\&.filters\&.do_center (str value, int  width = \fC80\fP)"

.PP
.nf
Centers the value in a field of a given width\&.
.fi
.PP
 
.SS " \fBV\fP jinja2\&.filters\&.do_default (\fBV\fP value, \fBV\fP  default_value = \fC''\fP, bool  boolean = \fCFalse\fP)"

.PP
.nf
If the value is undefined it will return the passed default value,
otherwise the value of the variable:

\&.\&. sourcecode:: jinja

    {{ my_variable|default('my_variable is not defined') }}

This will output the value of ``my_variable`` if the variable was
defined, otherwise ``'my_variable is not defined'``\&. If you want
to use default with variables that evaluate to false you have to
set the second parameter to `true`:

\&.\&. sourcecode:: jinja

    {{ ''|default('the string was empty', true) }}

\&.\&. versionchanged:: 2\&.11
   It's now possible to configure the :class:`~jinja2\&.Environment` with
   :class:`~jinja2\&.ChainableUndefined` to make the `default` filter work
   on nested elements and attributes that may contain undefined values
   in the chain without getting an :exc:`~jinja2\&.UndefinedError`\&.

.fi
.PP
 
.SS " t\&.List[t\&.Tuple[\fBK\fP, \fBV\fP]] jinja2\&.filters\&.do_dictsort (t\&.Mapping[\fBK\fP, \fBV\fP] value, bool  case_sensitive = \fCFalse\fP, 'te\&.Literal['key', 'value']'  by = \fC'key'\fP, bool  reverse = \fCFalse\fP)"

.PP
.nf
Sort a dict and yield (key, value) pairs\&. Python dicts may not
be in the order you want to display them in, so sort them first\&.

\&.\&. sourcecode:: jinja

    {% for key, value in mydict|dictsort %}
        sort the dict by key, case insensitive

    {% for key, value in mydict|dictsort(reverse=true) %}
        sort the dict by key, case insensitive, reverse order

    {% for key, value in mydict|dictsort(true) %}
        sort the dict by key, case sensitive

    {% for key, value in mydict|dictsort(false, 'value') %}
        sort the dict by value, case insensitive

.fi
.PP
 
.SS " str jinja2\&.filters\&.do_filesizeformat (t\&.Union[str, float, int] value, bool  binary = \fCFalse\fP)"

.PP
.nf
Format the value like a 'human-readable' file size (i\&.e\&. 13 kB,
4\&.1 MB, 102 Bytes, etc)\&.  Per default decimal prefixes are used (Mega,
Giga, etc\&.), if the second parameter is set to `True` the binary
prefixes are used (Mebi, Gibi)\&.

.fi
.PP
 
.SS " 't\&.Union[\fBV\fP, \fBUndefined\fP]' jinja2\&.filters\&.do_first ('Environment' environment, 't\&.Union[t\&.AsyncIterable[\fBV\fP], t\&.Iterable[\fBV\fP]]'
 seq)"

.SS " float jinja2\&.filters\&.do_float (t\&.Any value, float  default = \fC0\&.0\fP)"

.PP
.nf
Convert the value into a floating point number\&. If the
conversion doesn't work it will return ``0\&.0``\&. You can
override this default using the first parameter\&.

.fi
.PP
 
.SS " \fBMarkup\fP jinja2\&.filters\&.do_forceescape ('t\&.Union[str, \fBHasHTML\fP]' value)"

.PP
.nf
Enforce HTML escaping\&.  This will probably double escape variables\&.
.fi
.PP
 
.SS " str jinja2\&.filters\&.do_format (str value, *t\&.Any args, **t\&.Any kwargs)"

.PP
.nf
Apply the given values to a `printf-style`_ format string, like
``string % values``\&.

\&.\&. sourcecode:: jinja

    {{ '%s, %s!'|format(greeting, name) }}
